<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-complex-case" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Case of Complex Conjugate Eigenvalues</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       RECAP
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    On the last page, we found that our system
    <md>
      <mrow>x_1' \amp= 4x_1 - 3x_2</mrow>
      <mrow>x_2' \amp= 3x_1 + 4x_2</mrow>
    </md>
    has a solution
    <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -i \end{bmatrix}e^{(4 + 3i)t}</m>, but it is complex-valued,
    and we need two real solutions <m>\mathbf{y}(t)</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}(t)</m>. Extracting them
    requires some complex algebra, so let's warm up with the two things we will need.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CHECKPOINT 1: SPLITTING THE EXPONENTIAL
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-exponent-rule-complex">
    <title>Splitting the Exponential</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Our solution involves <m>e^{(4 + 3i)t} = e^{4t + (3t)i}</m>.
        Based on the exponent rules that hold for real numbers, which of the following
        would you expect to be true?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>e^{4t + (3t)i} = e^{4t}e^{(3t)i}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The exponent rule <m>e^{a + b} = e^{a}e^{b}</m> carries over to
            complex numbers, with <m>a = 4t</m> and <m>b = (3t)i</m>. A sum in the
            exponent splits into a product of exponentials.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>e^{4t + (3t)i} = e^{4t} + e^{(3t)i}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful: the exponent rule is <m>e^{a + b} = e^{a}e^{b}</m>. A sum in the
            exponent becomes a product of exponentials, not a sum. For instance,
            with real numbers, <m>e^{1 + 1} = e^2 = e \cdot e</m>, which is not
            <m>e + e</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>e^{4t + (3t)i} = e^{4t}e^{3t}e^{i}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful: <m>(3t)i</m> is a single <em>product</em>, not a sum, so it cannot be
            split into separate exponentials. The exponent rule
            <m>e^{a + b} = e^{a}e^{b}</m> only splits at the <m>+</m> sign, giving
            <m>e^{4t}e^{(3t)i}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EULER'S FORMULA
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The checkpoint leaves us with the factor <m>e^{(3t)i}</m>, an exponential with an
    imaginary exponent. Euler's formula, which we actually used back in Chapter 3
    for complex roots of characteristic equations, gives us a useful way of re-writing this expression.
  </p>

  <fact xml:id="fact-eulers-formula">
    <title>Euler's Formula</title>
    <p>
      For any <m>\theta</m>:
      <me>e^{i\theta} = \cos(\theta) + i\sin(\theta).</me>
      In particular, for a complex eigenvalue <m>\lambda = p + qi</m>:
      <me>e^{(p + qi)t} = e^{pt}e^{(qt)i} = e^{pt}\left(\cos(qt) + i\sin(qt)\right).</me>
    </p>
  </fact>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CHECKPOINT 2: DISTRIBUTING WITH i
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Applying Euler's formula with <m>\theta = 3t</m> to the expression we just simplified
    in the checkpoint:
    <me>e^{(4 + 3i)t} = e^{4t}e^{(3t)i} = e^{4t}\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right).</me>
    Expressions like <m>\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)</m> will
    need to be multiplied into the components of the eigenvector. One more warm-up before the main example!
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-foil-with-i">
    <title>Multiplying Out with <m>i</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Distribute and simplify
        <m>(-i)\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right)</m>. Which of the following is the
        correct result?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\sin(3t) - i\cos(3t)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Distributing,
            <m>(-i)\cos(3t) + (-i)(i)\sin(3t) = -i\cos(3t) - i^2\sin(3t)</m>, and since
            <m>i^2 = -1</m>, the last term is <m>+\sin(3t)</m>. Collecting the real part
            first: <m>\sin(3t) - i\cos(3t)</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>-\sin(3t) - i\cos(3t)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Watch the <m>i^2</m>! Distributing gives
            <m>-i\cos(3t) - i^2\sin(3t)</m>, and <m>i^2 = -1</m>, so the last term is
            <m>-(-1)\sin(3t) = +\sin(3t)</m>, not <m>-\sin(3t)</m>. The two negatives
            cancel.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>-i\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful when distributing: the second term is
            <m>(-i)(i\sin(3t)) = -i^2\sin(3t)</m>. The two factors of <m>i</m> multiply
            together, and <m>-i^2 = 1</m>, so this term is <m>+\sin(3t)</m>, with no
            <m>i</m> left in it at all.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       STRATEGY BLOCK
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now that we have established the relevant complex algebra tricks, let's take a look at the full strategy for 
    solving systems of ODEs with complex eigenvalues.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-complex-strategy">
    <title>The Eigenvalue Method for Complex Conjugate Eigenvalues</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      To solve <m>\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = A\mathbf{x}</m> when <m>A</m> has complex
      conjugate eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1, \lambda_2</m> with corresponding eigenvectors
      <m>\mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2</m>:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write down the complex-valued solution <m>\mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Apply Euler's formula and separate into real and imaginary parts:
            <me>\mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t} = \mathbf{y} + i\mathbf{z},</me>
            where <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> contain no <m>i</m> at all. Then
            it must be that <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> are themselves
            real-valued solutions, and they are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The general solution (for all real-valued solutions) is
            <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}.</me>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MAIN EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p>Alright, we can finally
 solve the system in the example below! </p>
  <example xml:id="ex-complex-general-solution">
    <title>Solving a System with Complex Conjugate Eigenvalues</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the general solution of the system
        <md>
          <mrow>x_1' \amp= 4x_1 - 3x_2</mrow>
          <mrow>x_2' \amp= 3x_1 + 4x_2.</mrow>
        </md>
        You may use the eigenvalue and eigenvector we found on the last page:
        <m>\lambda_1 = 4 + 3i</m> with
        <m>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -i \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We follow the three steps. Step 1 is quick: the complex-valued solution is
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -i \end{bmatrix}e^{(4 + 3i)t}.</me>
        Step 2 is where the work happens, so we break it into two parts, with the details
        of each in a dropdown.
      </p>
      <solution> <title>Details for Step 2a: Apply Euler's formula and multiply out</title>
      <p>
        We apply Euler's formula to the exponential:
        <me>e^{(4 + 3i)t} = e^{4t}\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right),</me>
        so
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t)
          = e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -i \end{bmatrix}\left(\cos(3t) + i\sin(3t)\right)
          = e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \cos(3t) + i\sin(3t) \\ -i\cos(3t) - i^2\sin(3t) \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Using <m>i^2 = -1</m>, the second component becomes
        <m>\sin(3t) - i\cos(3t)</m>.
      </p>
      </solution>
      <p>
        Step 2a leaves us with
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t)
          = e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \cos(3t) + i\sin(3t) \\ \sin(3t) - i\cos(3t) \end{bmatrix},</me>
        which no longer has any <m>i</m> in the exponent, so we can separate the
        components.
      </p>
      <solution> <title>Details for Step 2b: Separate into real and imaginary parts</title>
      <p>
        We separate everything with an <m>i</m> from everything without:
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t)
          = e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \cos(3t) + i\sin(3t) \\ \sin(3t) - i\cos(3t) \end{bmatrix}
          = \underbrace{e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \cos(3t) \\ \sin(3t) \end{bmatrix}}_{\mathbf{y}}
          + i\underbrace{e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \sin(3t) \\ -\cos(3t) \end{bmatrix}}_{\mathbf{z}}.</me>
      </p>
      </solution>
      <p>
        Step 2b identifies our two real-valued solutions:
        <me>\mathbf{y} = e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \cos(3t) \\ \sin(3t) \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \mathbf{z} = e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \sin(3t) \\ -\cos(3t) \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Step 3: the general solution is
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}
          = c_1 e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \cos(3t) \\ \sin(3t) \end{bmatrix}
          + c_2 e^{4t}\begin{bmatrix} \sin(3t) \\ -\cos(3t) \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Notice there is no <m>i</m> anywhere in the final answer!
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NOTE: ANSWERS CAN VARY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <note xml:id="note-complex-answers-vary">
    <title>Answers Can Look Very Different</title>
    <p>
      We only used one eigenvalue-eigenvector pair, <m>\lambda_1</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>; the conjugate pair <m>\lambda_2</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> is
      never needed (though using it instead also works). Because of this choice, and
      because eigenvectors themselves are only determined up to a multiple, correct answers
      for <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> can look quite different from each other.
      If your answer doesn't match a friend's, that alone doesn't mean either of you is
      wrong!
    </p>
  </note>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Euler's formula removes <m>i</m> from the exponent:</alert> for
          <m>\lambda = p + qi</m>, we have
          <m>e^{(p+qi)t} = e^{pt}(\cos(qt) + i\sin(qt))</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The three steps:</alert> write the complex solution
          <m>\mathbf{v}_1 e^{\lambda_1 t}</m>, separate it into real and imaginary parts
          <m>\mathbf{y} + i\mathbf{z}</m> using Euler's formula, and form the general
          solution <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Simplify with <m>i^2 = -1</m>:</alert> when multiplying out the
          components, terms with two factors of <m>i</m> become real.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>No <m>i</m> in the final answer:</alert> the general solution
          <m>c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}</m> is entirely real-valued. There should be no <m>i</m>
          appearing in your final answer.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="video-complex-case">
    <caption>This video goes through the content of this page.</caption>
    <video youtube="KAa9G_8jfqI" width="100%" preview="default"/>
  </figure>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-5-4" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.5.4" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

</section>
